The wave packet gives us an important lesson that we will meet again many times: in quantum mechanics, a particle with a definite position is a superposition of states with different momenta, and vice versa. The states with a definite energy, the solutions of the TISEq, are stationary and do not move. Motion requires a superposition of different stationary states, as we will see again in Chapter 6.

5.2 The Radial Wave Functions
The radial wave functions for the first three energy levels are listed in the box below. They all have the same structure: a polynomial in \(r\) multiplied by a decaying exponential, \(e^{-r/na_0}\). The exponential makes sure that the wave function goes to zero far from the nucleus, and it decays more slowly for larger \(n\), so orbitals with larger \(n\) extend farther out. The polynomial is responsible for the nodes.






NoteDetails: the radial wave functions





For any \(n\) and \(\ell\), the radial functions can be written as:
\[
R_{n\ell}(r) = - \left[ \frac{(n - \ell - 1)!}{2n[(n+\ell)!]^3} \right]^{1/2}\left(\frac{2}{na_0}\right)^{\ell+3/2}r^{\ell} e^{-r/na_0} L_{n+\ell}^{2\ell+1}\left( \frac{2r}{n a_0} \right),
\tag{5.11}\]
where \(L_{n+\ell}^{2\ell+1}\) are polynomials called associated Laguerre polynomials. Working out these polynomials and the normalization constants for the first three energy levels, \(n=1,2,3\), gives (the overall sign, which has no physical meaning, has been chosen to make each function positive near the nucleus):
\[
\begin{aligned}
R_{10}(r) &= 2\left(\frac{1}{a_0}\right)^{3/2} e^{-r/a_0} && (1s),\\
R_{20}(r) &= \frac{1}{2\sqrt{2}}\left(\frac{1}{a_0}\right)^{3/2}\left(2 - \frac{r}{a_0}\right) e^{-r/2a_0} && (2s),\\
R_{21}(r) &= \frac{1}{2\sqrt{6}}\left(\frac{1}{a_0}\right)^{3/2}\frac{r}{a_0}\, e^{-r/2a_0} && (2p),\\
R_{30}(r) &= \frac{2}{81\sqrt{3}}\left(\frac{1}{a_0}\right)^{3/2}\left[27 - 18\frac{r}{a_0} + 2\left(\frac{r}{a_0}\right)^2\right] e^{-r/3a_0} && (3s),\\
R_{31}(r) &= \frac{8}{27\sqrt{6}}\left(\frac{1}{a_0}\right)^{3/2} \frac{r}{a_0} \left(1 - \frac{r}{6a_0}\right) e^{-r/3a_0} && (3p),\\
R_{32}(r) &= \frac{4}{81\sqrt{30}}\left(\frac{1}{a_0}\right)^{3/2} \left(\frac{r}{a_0}\right)^2 e^{-r/3a_0} && (3d).
\end{aligned}
\]



The radial wave functions are plotted in Fig. 5.2. Let’s look at them:

Nodes. \(R_{10}\) (\(1s\)) is always positive. \(R_{20}\) (\(2s\)) changes sign at \(r=2a_0\), where the polynomial \(2-r/a_0\) is zero: this is a radial node, a sphere around the nucleus on which the wave function is zero. As for the particle in a box, higher energy means more nodes. In general, \(R_{n\ell}\) has \(n-\ell-1\) radial nodes.
The nucleus. The \(s\) functions are largest at the nucleus, while all the others start at zero, because of the factor \(r^\ell\): the rotational term in Eq. 5.6 keeps electrons with angular momentum away from the nucleus.
Angular nodes. The radial nodes are not the only ones. The angular part, \(Y_\ell^{m_\ell}\), adds \(\ell\) more (Chapter 4), so that, as for all the other systems we have studied, the total number of nodes grows with the energy: \((n-\ell-1)+\ell=n-1\). For example, \(2s\) has one radial node and no angular nodes, \(2p\) has no radial nodes and one angular node, and \(3d\) has two angular nodes. (As we saw for the particle on a sphere, the angular nodes are those of the real orbitals that we will meet below.)







Figure 5.2: The radial wave functions of the hydrogen atom orbitals in the first three energy levels. The radial nodes are highlighted with purple dots.



How likely is the electron to be found at a certain distance from the nucleus? To answer this question, we need to add up the probability over all the points at distance \(r\), which form a thin spherical shell. The volume of the shell, \(4\pi r^2 dr\), grows with \(r\), and this gives the radial probability distribution:
\[
P(r) = r^2 |R_{n\ell}(r)|^2,
\tag{5.12}\]
(the factor \(4\pi\) is taken care of by the normalization of the spherical harmonics). \(P(r)\,dr\) is the probability of finding the electron between \(r\) and \(r+dr\), whatever the direction, and it is plotted in Fig. 5.3.






Figure 5.3: The radial probability functions of the hydrogen atom orbitals in the first three energy levels. The radial nodes are highlighted with purple dots.



Notice how different \(P(r)\) is from \(|R_{n\ell}|^2\). For the \(1s\) orbital, \(|R_{10}|^2\) is largest at the nucleus, but there is very little volume close to the nucleus, so \(P(r)\) is zero there. Its maximum, the most probable distance of the electron from the nucleus, is at exactly \(r=a_0\), the radius of the Bohr model, although now the electron can be found at any distance. The \(2s\) distribution has two maxima, at about \(0.76\,a_0\) and \(5.24\,a_0\), separated by the radial node at \(2a_0\): even though the \(2s\) electron is mostly found far from the nucleus, it has a small but important probability of being found close to it. We will see in Chapter 9 that this small inner maximum is what makes the \(2s\) orbital lower in energy than \(2p\) in atoms with more than one electron.
Try it yourself. Choose \(n\) and \(\ell\) to see the radial probability distribution, \(P(r)=r^2|R_{n\ell}(r)|^2\), of any hydrogen orbital. Notice how the number of maxima, \(n-\ell\), and the most probable distance change.

viewof hn = Inputs.range([1, 6], {step: 1, value: 3, label: "n (principal quantum number)"})
viewof hl = Inputs.range([0, 5], {step: 1, value: 0, label: "ℓ (must be smaller than n)"})
















hydrogenPlot = {
  const n = hn, l = Math.min(hl, n - 1);
  // generalized Laguerre polynomial L_k^a(x) by recurrence
  const lag = (k, a, x) =&gt; {
    if (k === 0) return 1;
    let L0 = 1, L1 = 1 + a - x;
    for (let j = 1; j &lt; k; j++) { const L2 = ((2 * j + 1 + a - x) * L1 - (j + a) * L0) / (j + 1); L0 = L1; L1 = L2; }
    return L1;
  };
  const rmax = 2 * n * n + 10 * n, N = 1200, data = [];
  for (let i = 0; i &lt;= N; i++) {
    const r = rmax * i / N, rho = 2 * r / n;
    const R = Math.pow(rho, l) * Math.exp(-rho / 2) * lag(n - l - 1, 2 * l + 1, rho);
    data.push({r, P: r * r * R * R});
  }
  const norm = data.reduce((s, d) =&gt; s + d.P, 0) * (rmax / N);
  data.forEach(d =&gt; d.P /= norm);
  const names = "spdfgh";
  return Plot.plot({style: {fontSize: "14px", color: "var(--bs-body-color)", background: "transparent"}, marginLeft: 60, marginBottom: 45, marginTop: 35, 
    height: 320, grid: true,
    x: {label: "r (Bohr radii, a₀)"}, y: {label: "P(r)"},
    caption: `Radial probability distribution of the ${n}${names[l]} orbital of hydrogen` + (hl &gt; n - 1 ? ` (ℓ reduced to ${l}, since ℓ ≤ n − 1)` : ""),
    marks: [Plot.areaY(data, {x: "r", y: "P", fill: "steelblue", fillOpacity: 0.25}), Plot.line(data, {x: "r", y: "P", stroke: "steelblue", strokeWidth: 2}), Plot.ruleY([0])]
  });
}

8.3 Heisenberg’s Uncertainty Principle
As we saw in Sec. 3.4.2, a free particle described by a single plane wave, \(\psi(x)=Ae^{ipx/\hbar}\), has a perfectly defined momentum, but it is spread over all of space, so we cannot say where it is. To describe a particle that is localized, we need a wave packet: a superposition of many plane waves with different momenta, which interfere constructively in a small region of space and cancel everywhere else (Fig. 3.3). Written as a continuous sum over momenta, a wave packet is:
\[
\psi (x)=\frac {1}{\sqrt{2\pi\hbar}}\int_{-\infty }^{\infty }\varphi (p) \exp
\left(\frac{ip x}{\hbar} \right)\,dp,
\tag{8.3}\]
where \(\varphi(p)\) gives the amplitude of each plane wave in the packet. The function \(\varphi(p)\) is called the wave function in momentum space, and \(|\varphi(p)|^2\) is the probability density of finding the particle with momentum \(p\), just as \(|\psi(x)|^2\) is the probability density of finding it at position \(x\). In mathematical terms, \(\varphi(p)\) is the Fourier transform of \(\psi(x)\), and \(x\) and \(p\) are called conjugate variables. The two functions contain exactly the same information, written in two different ways, and they cannot both be narrow: localizing the particle in space requires a broad mixture of momenta, and vice versa. This connection is shown in Fig. 8.4 for three wave packets of different width.






Figure 8.4: Probability densities in position space (left) and in momentum space (right) for three wave packets (in units where \(\hbar=1\)). As the packet becomes narrower in position (from top to bottom), it becomes broader in momentum, and the product of the two standard deviations stays equal to \(\hbar/2\).



Try it yourself. Change the width of the packet and see how \(\sigma_x\) and \(\sigma_p\) change (in units where \(\hbar=1\)). For a single Gaussian packet, the product \(\sigma_x\sigma_p\) is always exactly \(\hbar/2\), the smallest value allowed. Now split the packet into two humps: the product grows, but it can never go below \(\hbar/2\).

viewof sxu = Inputs.range([0.1, 10], {step: 0.05, value: 1, label: "width of each hump"})
viewof du = Inputs.range([0, 4], {step: 0.1, value: 0, label: "separation of the two humps"})
















{
  // Two Gaussian humps, psi(x) = g(x - a) + g(x + a), with g(x) = exp(-x^2/(4 s^2)) and hbar = 1.
  // Everything is analytic: phi(p) is proportional to exp(-s^2 p^2) cos(p a).
  const s = sxu, a = du / 2, c = Math.exp(-a * a / (2 * s * s)), v = 1 / (4 * s * s);
  const sx = Math.sqrt(s * s + a * a / (1 + c));
  const sp = Math.sqrt(v - 4 * a * a * v * v * c / (1 + c));
  const g2 = (x) =&gt; Math.exp(-x * x / (2 * s * s));
  const Px = (x) =&gt; (g2(x - a) + g2(x + a) + 2 * c * g2(x)) / (Math.sqrt(2 * Math.PI) * s * (2 + 2 * c));
  const Pp = (p) =&gt; Math.exp(-2 * s * s * p * p) * Math.cos(p * a) ** 2 / (Math.sqrt(2 * Math.PI * v) * (1 + c) / 2);
  // fixed axes, so that the curves really get narrower or wider; extra points where the curves are sharp
  const XM = 35, PM = 18;
  const dense = (lo, hi, n) =&gt; d3.range(n + 1).map(k =&gt; lo + (hi - lo) * k / n);
  const xs = [...dense(-XM, XM, 1200), ...dense(-a - 6 * s, -a + 6 * s, 300), ...dense(a - 6 * s, a + 6 * s, 300)].filter(x =&gt; Math.abs(x) &lt;= XM).sort((m, n) =&gt; m - n);
  const ps = [...dense(-PM, PM, 1200), ...dense(-3 / s, 3 / s, 400)].filter(p =&gt; Math.abs(p) &lt;= PM).sort((m, n) =&gt; m - n);
  // y axes: from 0 to 10% above the highest point of each curve, so the peak always reaches about 90% of the height
  const datX = xs.map(x =&gt; ({x, P: Px(x)})), datP = ps.map(p =&gt; ({p, P: Pp(p)}));
  const yX = 1.1 * d3.max(datX, d =&gt; d.P), yP = 1.1 * d3.max(datP, d =&gt; d.P);
  const narrow = width &lt; 600, w = narrow ? width : Math.floor((width - 16) / 2);
  const p1 = Plot.plot({style: {fontSize: "14px", color: "var(--bs-body-color)", background: "transparent"}, marginLeft: 60, marginBottom: 45, marginTop: 35, width: w, height: 250, grid: true, x: {label: "position x", domain: [-XM, XM]}, y: {label: "|ψ(x)|²", domain: [0, yX], nice: false}, caption: `σₓ = ${sx.toFixed(2)}`,
    marks: [Plot.areaY(datX, {x: "x", y: "P", fill: "#1f77b4", fillOpacity: 0.3}), Plot.line(datX, {x: "x", y: "P", stroke: "#1f77b4", strokeWidth: 2}), Plot.ruleY([0])]});
